UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 2: Dance steps and rhythm', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat. We can show rhythm with claps, steps or dance moves, such as slow, slow, quick-quick. Dance steps such as step-touch, marching and skipping can be combined into patterns. Keep time with the beat, repeat patterns so they become smooth and leave space between dancers.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a rhythm pattern using the words ''slow'' and ''quick'' and say what step you would use for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward. A rhythm pattern uses long and short movements over a steady beat.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking beat and rhythm are the same. The beat is steady; the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that happens on top of it.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a rhythm pattern with at least four steps using slow and quick words and write it as a chart. Perform it over a steady beat five times, then change one step. Record which version flowed better and why.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat. We can show rhythm with claps, steps or dance moves, such as slow, slow, quick-quick. Dance steps such as step-touch, marching and skipping can be combined into patterns. Keep time with the beat, repeat patterns so they become smooth and leave space between dancers.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a rhythm pattern with at least four steps using slow and quick words and write it as a chart. Perform it over a steady beat five times, then change one step. Record which version flowed better and why. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using rhythm on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking beat and rhythm are the same.. Correction: Sample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking beat and rhythm are the same. The beat is steady; the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that happens on top of it. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating rhythm on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with rhythm, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about rhythm with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rhythm. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for rhythm. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking beat and rhythm are the same","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward"],"question":"Write a rhythm pattern using the words ''slow'' and ''quick'' and say what step you would use for each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for rhythm; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat","Sample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward","Careful use of slow and quick and explain why it matter with clear evidence","Thinking beat and rhythm are the same"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with rhythm?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named slow and quick and explain why it matter, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking beat and rhythm are the same","Name slow and quick and explain why it matter only, without linking it to rhythm","Apply rhythm to new material and name the slow and quick and explain why it matter used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies rhythm with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on rhythm. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking beat and rhythm are the same"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for rhythm?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking beat and rhythm are the same","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with rhythm","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about rhythm?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking beat and rhythm are the same","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rhythm?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for rhythm?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name slow and quick and explain why it matter only, with no linked example","Create a rhythm pattern with at least four steps using slow and quick words and write it as a chart","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking beat and rhythm are the same"],"question":"Which independent response best applies rhythm to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about rhythm with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating rhythm on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using rhythm on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rhythm. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with rhythm, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking beat and rhythm are the same.. Correction: Sample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w33-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rhythm.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use rhythm with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify slow and quick and explain why it matters.","I can practise dance steps independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a pattern needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rhythm with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step-touch, slow step back. Repeat the pattern four times over a steady beat. Meg says the rhythm words aloud to help her feet.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a rhythm pattern using the words ''slow'' and ''quick'' and say what step you would use for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward. A rhythm pattern uses long and short movements over a steady beat.","explain":"Rhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat. We can show rhythm with claps, steps or dance moves, such as slow, slow, quick-quick. Dance steps such as step-touch, marching and skipping can be combined into patterns. Keep time with the beat, repeat patterns so they become smooth and leave space between dancers.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking beat and rhythm are the same. The beat is steady; the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that happens on top of it. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Rhythm over a beat.","caption":"Rhythm sits on the beat.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow, slow, quick-quick</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">steady underneath</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rhythm with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step-touch, slow step back. Repeat the pattern four times over a steady beat. Meg says the rhythm words aloud to help her feet. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Dance steps and space.","caption":"Keep a safe gap.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Steps</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">step-touch, march, skip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">leave room between dancers</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rhythm with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking beat and rhythm are the same. The beat is steady; the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that happens on top of it. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step-touch, slow step back. Repeat the pattern four times over a steady beat. Meg says the rhythm words aloud to help her feet.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''quick-quick, slow, slow'': two quick steps on the spot, then two big slow steps forward. A rhythm pattern uses long and short movements over a steady beat.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking beat and rhythm are the same. The beat is steady; the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that happens on top of it. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rhythm with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRhythm is a pattern of long and short sounds or movements over the beat. We can show rhythm with claps, steps or dance moves, such as slow, slow, quick-quick. Dance steps such as step-touch, marching and skipping can be combined into patterns. Keep time with the beat, repeat patterns so they become smooth and leave space between dancers. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 2: Dance steps and rhythm","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Gymnastics and dance","week":33,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a rhythm pattern ''slow, slow, quick-quick, slow'' using steps. Slow step right, slow step left, quick-quick step-touch, slow step back. Repeat the pattern four times over a steady beat. Meg says the rhythm words aloud to help her feet.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Rhythm over a beat.","caption":"Rhythm sits on the beat.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">slow, slow, quick-quick</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">steady underneath</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Dance steps and space.","caption":"Keep a safe gap.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Steps</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">step-touch, march, skip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">leave room between dancers</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w33-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
